  "pd": {
    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "The melody is a 19th-century Sadigura Hasidic nigun from Bukovina, in circulation long before 1900; Abraham Zvi Idelsohn notated and set it in Jerusalem around 1918, and it was in print and on commercial record in the early 1920s - well before 1930 either way.",
    "sourceEdition": "Public-domain reference notation of the Idelsohn setting: melody line only, G minor, 4/4, harmonic-minor (Ahava Rabba) inflection",
    "notes": "Melody and changes only, no lyrics - which also sidesteps the disputed authorship of the Hebrew words. The traditional nigun setting is encoded, not any later concert or dance-band arrangement. Fermatas on the closing bars are flattened onto the bar grid so the section loops; the accelerando is left to the player."
  },
